0.25 w BT/F4 10 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 316.901 131.75 TD [ (1-)1 (1)] TJ/F7 24 Tf-172.776 491.25 TD (1. Introduction)Tj/F7 14 Tf0 -45.25 TD (1.1. An Overview of PDQUEST)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (PDQUEST is a software package for imaging, analyzing, and databasing 2-D )Tj0 -12 TD (electrophoresis gels.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.016 Tc (The software runs in a Windows environment and has an easy-to-use )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (graphical interface with standard pull-down menus, toolbars, and keyboard )TjT* (commands.)Tj0 -20 TD (PDQUEST collects data from gels scanned with one of several Bio-Rad )Tj0 -12 TD (imaging systems. An image of a sample is captured using the controls in the )TjT* (imaging device window and displayed on your computer monitor. Image )TjT* (processing and analysis operations are performed using commands from the )TjT* (menus and toolbars.)Tj0 -20 TD (Once a gel has been scanned, advanced algorithms are available to remove )Tj0 -12 TD (background noise, gel artifacts, and horizontal or vertical streaking from the )TjT* (image. PDQUEST then uses a spot segmentation facility to detect and )TjT* (quantify protein spots. Protein spots that change over time can be traced, )TjT* (quanti\336ed, displayed on-screen, and exported to other applications for )TjT* (statistical analysis.)Tj0 -20 TD (The software can be used to simultaneously analyze up to 10,000 spots on a )Tj0 -12 TD (given 2-D gel. PDQUEST can track and report all of the protein pattern and )TjT* (concentration changes in the sample, making feasible both qualitative and )TjT* (quantitative analysis of the data generated from 2-D gels.)Tj0 -20 TD (The spot matching and databasing facilities make it possible to objectively )Tj0 -12 TD (compare hundreds of different gels. Histograms allow you to quickly )TjT* (compare the amount of the same spot in each gel within an experimental )TjT* -0.166 Tw (series. Spots can be grouped into user-de\336ned sets. Higher level comparisons )TjT* -0.269 Tw (and analysis can be performed on master images that you generate from your )TjT* 0 Tw (experimental gels.)Tj0 -20 TD (Scan \336les can be transferred into or exported out of Discovery Series)Tj/F5 10 Tf301.096 0 TD ( )Tj/F6 10 Tf-301.096 -12 TD (applications. Scans can be converted into TIFF format for easy compatibility )TjETendstreamendobj48 0 obj2334endobj46 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 38 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F5 18 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 47 0 R>>endobj50 0 obj<</Length 51 0 R>>stream
0.25 w BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (PDQUEST User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf172.776 -530.75 TD [ (1-)1 (2)] TJ/F6 10 Tf-154.776 500.5 TD (with other applications. Match data can be exported into spreadsheets for )Tj0 -12 TD (easy analysis.)Tj/F7 14 Tf-18 -38.5 TD (1.2. A Brief Overview of 2-D Gel Electrophoresis)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (2-D gel electrophoresis combines the techniques of isoelectric focusing with )Tj0 -12 TD (SDS polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis. Since these two separation methods )TjT* -0.484 Tw (rely on independent properties of proteins, chemical \(IEF\) and physical \(SDS\), )TjT* 0 Tw (this procedure can resolve complex biological samples and polypeptide )TjT* -0.018 Tc (mixes. The extraordinary resolution capabilities of SDS-PAGE make it useful )TjT* 0 Tc (for studying complex systems in great detail.)Tj0 -20 TD (2-D gels generate high resolution separation of proteins. The isoelectric )Tj0 -12 TD (focusing \(IEF\) dimension is most often run in cylindrical tube gels. After )TjT* -0.429 Tw (focusing, the cylindrical gel is placed on top of an SDS-PAGE slab gel which is )TjT* 0 Tw (then run as usual. Proteins can be visualized by metabolic radiolabeling or a )TjT* (variety of staining methods.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-9 -27.5 TD (Gene Expression Studies)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (2-D gel electrophoresis is an excellent technique for the study of differential )Tj0 -12 TD (gene expression under various growth conditions. Since the expression and )TjT* -0.356 Tw (regulation of individual proteins can be detected, 2-D gels are an indirect way )TjT* -0.015 Tc 0 Tw (to monitor gene activity. They allow for the investigation of quantitative as )TjT* 0 Tc (well as qualitative changes in cellular protein expression.)Tj0 -20 TD (The environmental conditions of a cell can be changed in order to determine )Tj0 -12 TD (optimal growth conditions as well as monitor the cell\325s response to different )TjT* (stresses. Environmental conditions that can stress the cell include changes in )TjT* -0.019 Tc (temperature, pH and nutrient availability. Some examples of the chemical )TjT* 0 Tc (stresses that can be placed on a cell are drug and hormone treatments. Since )TjT* -0.402 Tw (protein structure and function are the direct result of gene expression, the loss )TjT* 0 Tw (or change of a protein as detected by a 2-D gel can be extrapolated back to )TjT* (events occurring at the DNA level.)Tj0 -20 TD (Many questions encountered when genes are inserted and expressed in )Tj0 -12 TD (bacteria, yeast, and other cell types can be answered with 2-D analysis: Is the )TjT* (cell making the protein? Is the cell\325s progeny making the protein? Is the )TjT* (protein being made but not secreted? Have mutations occurred?)TjETendstreamendobj51 0 obj2792endobj49 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 38 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 50 0 R>>endobj53 0 obj<</Length 54 0 R>>stream
0.25 w BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 436.126 662.5 TD (Introduction)Tj/F4 10 Tf-119.224 -530.75 TD [ (1-)1 (3)] TJ/F7 11 Tf-163.776 500 TD (Medical Applications)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (2-D gels can also have important applications in medical research. For )Tj0 -12 TD (example, this technique can be used to verify the presence of speci\336c protein )TjT* (markers that are linked to genetic diseases and disease states. Used in )TjT* (conjunction with other tests, 2-D gels can be part of medical screening )TjT* (procedures associated with mutations and teratology linked to genetic )TjT* (damage.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.468 Tw (Growth factors are being studied for their role in the regulation of cell growth. )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (2-D gels can be used to evaluate quantitative and qualitative changes in )TjT* (cellular proteins in response to growth factor stimulation.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.151 Tw (2-D gels allow the visualization of proteins whose expression is altered as the )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (result of cell transformation, introducing oncogenes into the host genome. )TjT* (Assessment of phosphorylation, sulfation, or other secondary modi\336cations )TjT* (could reveal functional protein pathways affected by oncogene expression. )TjT* (This information could contribute to a better understanding of cell growth )TjT* (and regulation.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-9 -27.5 TD (Sample Experiments)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD -0.014 Tc (Valuable information can be learned by exposing cells to a set of speci\336c )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (experimental conditions and subsequently examining their biological )TjT* (response.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.39 Tw (A preliminary in vitro experiment that is useful when beginning 2-D gel work )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (is to radiolabel a cell\325s proteins to steady-state with )Tj/F6 8 Tf228.091 4 TD (35)Tj/F6 10 Tf8 -4 TD (S-methionine. Running )Tj-236.091 -12 TD -0.491 Tw (gels of these proteins will help to determine the commonly expressed proteins )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (in the cell under normal conditions.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.028 Tc (Subsequently, other amino acids such as )Tj/F6 8 Tf179.917 4 TD (3)Tj/F6 10 Tf4 -4 TD 0 Tc (H-leucine, )Tj/F6 8 Tf48.32 4 TD (3)Tj/F6 10 Tf4 -4 TD (H-proline, )Tj/F6 8 Tf48.301 4 TD (3)Tj/F6 10 Tf4 -4 TD (H-lysine, )Tj-288.538 -12 TD (etc., can be used to radiolabel the proteins to steady-state. This will label any )Tj0 -12 TD (proteins that do not contain methionine and were, therefore, not detected in )TjT* (the \336rst experiment, and will provide preliminary information on the amino )TjT* (acid composition.)Tj0 -20 TD (Other experiments complementary to 2-D gels include: cell fractionation )Tj0 -12 TD (procedures, and post-translational modi\336cations \(phosphorylation, )TjT* (methylation, etc.\). Data from these experiments can be added to a database, )TjT* (accumulating information about these proteins.)TjETendstreamendobj54 0 obj2889endobj52 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 38 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 53 0 R>>endobj57 0 obj<</Length 58 0 R>>stream
0.25 w BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (PDQUEST User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf172.776 -530.75 TD [ (1-)1 (4)] TJ/F7 14 Tf-172.776 498 TD (1.3. Digital Data and Signal Intensity)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (The Bio-Rad imaging devices supported by PDQUEST are light and/or )Tj0 -12 TD (radiation detectors that convert signals from biological samples into digital )TjT* (data. PDQUEST then displays the digital data on your computer screen, in )TjT* (the form of gray scale or color images.)Tj0 -20 TD (A data object as displayed on the computer is composed of tiny individual )Tj0 -12 TD -0.108 Tw (screen pixels. Each pixel has an X and Y coordinate, and a value Z. The X and )TjT* 0 Tw (Y coordinates are the pixel\325s horizontal and vertical positions on the image, )TjT* (and the Z value is the signal intensity of the pixel.)Tj/F10 9 Tf13.97 -181.25 TD (Fig.1-1. Representation of the pixels in two digitally imaged bands in a gel.)Tj/F6 10 Tf-13.97 -21.75 TD (For a data object to be visible and quanti\336able, the intensity of its clustered )Tj0 -12 TD (pixels must be higher than the intensity of the pixels that make up the )TjT* (background of the image. The total intensity of a data object is the sum of the )TjT* (intensities of all the pixels that make up the object. The mean intensity of a )TjT* (data object is the total intensity divided by the number of pixels in the object.)Tj0 -20 TD (The units of signal intensity are Optical Density \(O.D.\) in the case of the GS-)Tj0 -12 TD (700 and 710 densitometers, the Gel Doc with a white light source, or the )TjT* (Fluor-S and Fluor-S MAX MultiImagers with white light illumination. Signal )TjT* (intensity is expressed in counts when using the Personal FX or FX, or in the )TjT* (case of the Gel Doc, Fluor-S, or Fluor-S MAX when using the UV light source.)TjET2 j 331.625 442.375 m 319.125 429.875 l 342.375 429.875 l 354.875 442.375 l 331.625 442.375 l s 0 j 1 g 331.5 440 1.75 14 ref 331.625 4