BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (PDQUEST User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf169.997 -530.75 TD [ (3-)1 (30)] TJ/F6 10 Tf-151.997 500.5 TD -0.172 Tw (amount of signal at each point in the range. \(This is the View > Plot Density > )Tj0 -12 TD -0.066 Tc 0 Tw (Plot Vertical Trace histogram.\))Tj0 -20 TD 0 Tc (This histogram can be used in conjunction with the Wizard to identify the )Tj0 -12 TD (type of noise in your image.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-9 -27.5 TD (Step I: Identify Noise Characteristics)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (The \336rst step in the Wizard is to identify the type of noise in your image. The )Tj0 -12 TD -0.019 Tc (Wizard allows you to select from a variety of different noise types and )TjT* 0 Tc (combinations.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.3 Tw (To determine the type of noise in your image, it is a good idea to \336rst magnify )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (a background region of the image \(i.e., a region where there is no data\) using )TjT* (the Zoom Box command on the Main toolbar. Note that as you magnify a )TjT* (region, the density distribution histogram changes to re\337ect only the signal )TjT* (within the region you have magni\336ed.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.331 Tw (After examining the magni\336ed region, select one, both, or neither of the check )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (boxes described below.)Tj0 -18 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Salt.)Tj/F6 10 Tf21.716 0 TD ( This type of noise appears as specks that are lighter than the )Tj-21.716 -12 TD -0.195 Tw (surrounding background. The density distribution histogram of this type )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (of noise displays noise peaks at the high end of the range. This type of )TjT* (noise is common in electronic cameras with malfunctioning pixels. It can )TjT* (also be caused by dust or lint in the imaging optics or scratches on )TjT* (photographic \336lm. Salt is a type of outlier noise \(see below\). )Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD 0.17 Tc (Pepper.)Tj/F6 10 Tf36.196 0 TD 0 Tc ( This type of noise appears as specks that are darker than the )Tj-36.196 -12 TD -0.358 Tw (surrounding background. The distribution histogram of this type of noise )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (displays noise peaks at the low end of the range. Its causes are similar to )TjT* (those of salt noise. Pepper is a type of outlier noise \(see below\).)Tj-18 -20 TD (Next, select one of the following option buttons to describe additional )Tj0 -12 TD (features of your noise.)Tj0 -18 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Gaussian.)Tj/F6 10 Tf47.864 0 TD -0.46 Tw ( The distribution histogram of this type of noise has a Gaussian )Tj-47.864 -12 TD 0 Tw (pro\336le, usually at the bottom of the data range. This type of noise is )Tj0 -12 TD (usually an electronic artifact created by cameras and sensors, or by a )TjT* (combination of independent unknown noise sources.)TjETendstreamendobj617 0 obj2802endobj615 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 563 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F5 18 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 616 0 R>>endobj620 0 obj<</Length 621 0 R>>stream
BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 357.422 662.5 TD (Viewing and Editing Images)Tj/F4 10 Tf-43.301 -530.75 TD [ (3-)1 (31)] TJ/F6 10 Tf-151.997 500.5 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Uniform noise.)Tj/F6 10 Tf72.704 0 TD -0.213 Tw ( This type of noise appears in the histogram as a uniform )Tj-72.704 -12 TD 0 Tw (layer of noise across the data range of the image.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Outlier noise. )Tj/F6 10 Tf69.387 0 TD (This category of noise includes salt and pepper noise \(see )Tj-69.387 -12 TD (above\). The distribution histogram of this type of noise displays noise )Tj0 -12 TD (peaks at the high and low ends of the range.)Tj-18 -20 TD (After you have identi\336ed the type of noise, go to Step 2.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-9 -27.5 TD (Step 2: Select Filter Size)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (Image noise is \336ltered by means of a \336ltering window \(or kernel\), which is )Tj0 -12 TD -0.109 Tw (measured in pixels. This \336ltering window slides across the image, processing )TjT* 0 Tw (the pixels within it.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.321 Tw (The available \336lter dimensions range from 3)Tj/F1 10 Tf193.29 0 TD 0 Tw ( x )Tj/F6 10 Tf9.93 0 TD (3 pixels to 9)Tj/F1 10 Tf51.381 0 TD ( x )Tj/F6 10 Tf9.93 0 TD -0.515 Tw (9 pixels. To select )Tj-264.532 -12 TD 0 Tw (an appropriate size, magnify a background region of your image so that you )Tj0 -12 TD (can see the individual pixels. The \336lter size you select should be larger than )TjT* (the average noise feature but smaller than your data features.)Tj/F12 9 Tf-18 -20 TD (Note:)Tj/F6 10 Tf36 0 TD -0.023 Tc (A smaller \336lter will alter your image less than a larger \336lter. Large \336lters )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (can result in better suppression of noise, but can also blur desirable )TjT* (features in the image.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-27 -27.5 TD (Step 3: Begin Filtering)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (After you have completed your selections, the \336lter name and size will be )Tj0 -12 TD (displayed at the bottom of the Filter Wizard dialog box.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.015 Tc (To being \336ltering, click on the OK button. Because \336ltering is an irreversible )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (process, a pop-up box will give you the option of \336ltering the original image, )TjT* (creating a copy of the image to \336lter, or cancelling out of this operation.)Tj0 -20 TD (If you choose to Copy and Filter, you will be asked to enter a new name and/)Tj0 -12 TD (or version number for the new copy before the operation is performed. Once )TjT* (this information has been entered, the \336ltering operation will be performed.)TjETendstreamendobj621 0 obj2587endobj618 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 619 0 R/Resources <</Font 622 0 R/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 620 0 R>>endobj622 0 obj<</F1 7 0 R/F4 16 0 R/F5 18 0 R/F6 20 0 R/F7 25 0 R/F12 74 0 R>>endobj624 0 obj<</Length 625 0 R>>stream
BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (PDQUEST User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf169.997 -530.75 TD [ (3-)1 (32)] TJ/F7 12 Tf-169.997 499.25 TD (3.11.b. Selecting a Filter Directly)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -20.75 TD (If you already know the type and size of \336lter you want, you can select it )Tj0 -12 TD (directly by selecting Image > Filters > Filter List. The pulldown menu )TjT* (includes all the \336lters available in the Discovery Series.)Tj0 -20 TD (The types of \336lters in the Discovery Series are:)Tj0 -18 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Weighted Mean.)Tj/F6 10 Tf78.865 0 TD ( This \336lter is useful for reducing Gaussian noise. It )Tj-78.865 -12 TD (calculates the weighted mean of the pixels within the \336ltering window )Tj0 -12 TD (and uses it to replace the value of the pixel being processed.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Out of Range Pixel.)Tj/F6 10 Tf93.864 0 TD -0.183 Tw ( This \336lter is useful for suppressing salt-and-pepper )Tj-93.864 -12 TD 0 Tw (noise; its effect on Gaussian noise is minimal. This \336lter calculates the )Tj0 -12 TD -0.466 Tw (mean of the pixel values in the \336ltering window, including the pixel being )TjT* 0 Tw (processed. If the difference between the mean and the individual pixel )TjT* (value is above a certain threshold, then the individual value is replaced )TjT* (by the mean.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Median.)Tj/F6 10 Tf40.078 0 TD ( Also useful for suppressing salt-and-pepper noise, this \336lter )Tj-40.078 -12 TD -0.123 Tw (calculates the median value of the pixels within the \336ltering window and )Tj0 -12 TD -0.147 Tw (uses it to replace the value of the pixel being processed. The median \336lter )TjT* 0 Tw (produces very little blurring if a small-sized window is selected.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Maximum.)Tj/F6 10 Tf52.039 0 TD ( This \336lter is useful for eliminating pepper noise in an image )Tj-52.039 -12 TD (\(it worsens the effect of salt noise\). It replaces the value of the pixel being )Tj0 -12 TD (processed with the maximum value of the pixels within the \336ltering )TjT* (window.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (Minimum.)Tj/F6 10 Tf51.475 0 TD -0.443 Tw ( This \336lter replaces the value of the pixel being processed with )Tj-51.475 -12 TD -0.197 Tw (the minimum pixel value within the \336ltering window. This \336lter is useful )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (for eliminating salt noise in an image \(it worsens the effect of pepper\).)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD (MidValue.)Tj/F6 10 Tf50.654 0 TD ( This \336lter is useful for suppressing uniform noise within an )Tj-50.654 -12 TD -0.015 Tc (image; however, it worsens the effect of pepper and salt. This \336lter )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (replaces the value of the pixel being processed with the mean of the )TjT* -0.017 Tc (maximum and minimum pixel values within the \336ltering window.)Tj-18 -16 TD (\245)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 0 TD 0 Tc (Power Mean.)Tj/F6 10 Tf62.826 0 TD -0.345 Tw ( This \336lter is useful for suppressing salt and Gaussian noise )Tj-62.826 -12 TD 0 Tw (within an image \(it worsens the effect of pepper noise\). It replaces the )Tj0 -12 TD (value of the pixel being processed with the power mean of the pixel )TjT* (values within the \336ltering window.)TjETendstreamendobj625 0 obj3298endobj623 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 619 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F5 18 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 624 0 R>>endobj627 0 obj<</Length 628 0 R>>stream
